  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. R1, R3, R5, and R6 already establish high-energy mechanical alloying of elemental HEA powders under argon followed by short-duration SPS, including milling-time-dependent hardness and phase evolution. R2 further links severe deformation, grain refinement, dislocation-related effects, low porosity, and hardness approaching 680 HV. R12 supplies especially relevant compositional evidence: a closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni-Ti alloy becomes dense and very hard after 1050 °C/10 min SPS. Other records independently connect nanocrystalline grains, high dislocation density, solid-solution or mixed-phase structures, and reduced porosity with high strength or hardness. Although none reports the exact equimolar CoCrFeNiTi composition, 1–6 h milling window, 900–1000 °C SPS schedule, or 83% FCC/17% BCC fraction, an expert could plausibly combine the established short-time milling-plus-SPS route with the known strengthening principles and closely related alloy evidence to propose the later intervention and mechanism."
